      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Defibrillation is indicated only for shock‑able rhythms (ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia); asystole is classified as a non‑shockable rhythm.",
      "evidence": "2020 AHA Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS) algorithm lists VF and pulseless VT as the only rhythms for which a shock is recommended; asystole and PEA are explicitly labeled non‑shockable.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because asystole has no electrical activity to reset, delivering a defibrillation shock provides no therapeutic benefit.",
      "evidence": "Clinical studies (e.g., Perkins et al., 2015) show no improvement in ROSC or survival when a shock is applied to asystole; guidelines therefore advise against it.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The recommended treatment for asystole is high‑quality CPR combined with epinephrine administration, not defibrillation.",
      "evidence": "ACLS protocol for asystole: immediate CPR, 1 mg epinephrine every 3–5 minutes, and continued reassessment; no shock is part of the algorithm.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "A potential counter‑argument that a shock might be attempted in asystole is refuted by evidence showing it wastes critical time without benefit.",
      "evidence": "Guideline commentary (AHA 2020) explicitly states that attempting a shock in asystole delays CPR and drug administration, worsening outcomes.",
      "weight": 0.00,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    }
  ],
